1
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:11,739
Trurl once built a machine that could do
one sum, two and two, and it said seven.

2
00:00:11,779 --> 00:00:14,552
After that, Klapaucius
never let him forget it.

3
00:00:15,252 --> 00:00:18,465
KLAPAUCIUS: How is the
arithmetic, Trurl? Still seven?

4
00:00:20,065 --> 00:00:24,203
TRURL: Then I will build a machine that
writes poetry. We will see who laughs.

5
00:00:25,203 --> 00:00:28,263
He bought eight hundred tons
of books on cybernetics and

6
00:00:28,303 --> 00:00:31,459
twelve thousand tons of verse,
and he read them in turns.

7
00:00:33,759 --> 00:00:35,023
Then the real problem.

8
00:00:35,063 --> 00:00:38,224
A poet’s program is written
by his whole civilization,

9
00:00:38,264 --> 00:00:41,228
and that by the one before,
back to the beginning.

10
00:00:42,128 --> 00:00:45,178
TRURL: To get one poet, I
must first make the universe.

11
00:00:45,978 --> 00:00:48,916
He modelled the void, then
light, then gas clouds,

12
00:00:48,956 --> 00:00:53,005
then the first ice age, a billion
events in a billionth of a second.

13
00:00:53,805 --> 00:00:55,506
History spooled onto reels.

14
00:00:55,546 --> 00:00:58,923
Twice it went wrong: a fuse
reversed the first crime,

15
00:00:58,963 --> 00:01:01,502
and a fly turned the apes into curtains.

16
00:01:01,542 --> 00:01:03,347
Trurl ran those parts again.

17
00:01:03,947 --> 00:01:06,804
In the end the reels stood
higher than anyone could see.

18
00:01:09,004 --> 00:01:12,452
For two weeks he fed in instructions.
Then he threw the switch.

19
00:01:13,352 --> 00:01:16,769
THE BARD: Today we consider
the grinding of crystal faces.

20
00:01:17,269 --> 00:01:19,253
TRURL: Too much logic. More feeling.

21
00:01:20,053 --> 00:01:22,175
THE BARD: Oh, what a cruel, cruel world.

22
00:01:22,575 --> 00:01:23,836
TRURL: Now some character.

23
00:01:24,436 --> 00:01:26,215
THE BARD: From now on you do as I say.

24
00:01:26,255 --> 00:01:28,839
First, add six floors, so I
can think about existence.

25
00:01:29,239 --> 00:01:31,298
TRURL: A throttle for philosophy, then.

26
00:01:32,698 --> 00:01:34,064
THE BARD: I had a little frog.

27
00:01:34,664 --> 00:01:36,238
The next poem was doggerel.

28
00:01:36,278 --> 00:01:39,644
He fitted filters of corundum
steel, and a rhyme circuit.

29
00:01:39,684 --> 00:01:42,254
The machine decided to
become a missionary.

30
00:01:42,954 --> 00:01:45,405
TRURL: Take out the logic. Put in vanity.

31
00:01:46,605 --> 00:01:49,328
THE BARD: Nobody understands
me. People are beasts.

32
00:01:49,368 --> 00:01:52,076
They will be sorry when I
am gone. Bring me paper.

33
00:01:52,876 --> 00:01:55,318
Trurl sighed, switched
it off, and went to bed.

34
00:01:57,518 --> 00:02:01,119
In the morning Klapaucius came,
eager to watch his friend fail.

35
00:02:01,719 --> 00:02:04,909
TRURL: It needs to warm up. Let
us start with something simple.

36
00:02:07,109 --> 00:02:08,161
THE BARD: Glimmerquox.

37
00:02:08,661 --> 00:02:09,546
Pendulate.

38
00:02:10,046 --> 00:02:10,660
Brumm.

39
00:02:11,660 --> 00:02:12,876
KLAPAUCIUS: Is that it?

40
00:02:13,376 --> 00:02:13,979
TRURL: Wait.

41
00:02:15,179 --> 00:02:17,215
THE BARD: Sorrel plinth a-wumbering so,

42
00:02:17,515 --> 00:02:19,115
Gaddle the frimmish oot;

43
00:02:19,415 --> 00:02:21,386
Narn ye quell the bobbin’s toe,

44
00:02:21,686 --> 00:02:23,326
Hallow, hallow, fluot!

45
00:02:24,326 --> 00:02:26,097
KLAPAUCIUS: Am I missing something?

46
00:02:33,497 --> 00:02:36,631
TRURL: One tube. All that
universe, and one tube.

47
00:02:37,831 --> 00:02:39,792
THE BARD: Ready. Whom shall I write for?

48
00:02:42,792 --> 00:02:46,130
KLAPAUCIUS: Very well. Something
for whoever laughed too soon.

49
00:02:47,530 --> 00:02:49,239
THE BARD: Who laughs before the proof

50
00:02:49,539 --> 00:02:51,413
Had best stand well aloof:

51
00:02:51,813 --> 00:02:55,412
The engine stumbled
once, and found its feet.

52
00:02:55,812 --> 00:02:57,384
The doubter on the floor

53
00:02:57,684 --> 00:02:59,186
May doubt a little more;

54
00:02:59,586 --> 00:03:02,209
He cannot make this couplet less complete.

55
00:03:03,109 --> 00:03:05,657
KLAPAUCIUS: A coincidence.
Something harder.

56
00:03:07,857 --> 00:03:13,510
A haircut. Lofty and tragic. Six
lines, every word starting with S.

57
00:03:15,110 --> 00:03:18,047
THE BARD: Sweet Sabine slept,
serenely silver-tressed;

58
00:03:18,447 --> 00:03:21,922
Sly Sela stalked, slow,
smiling, self-possessed,

59
00:03:22,322 --> 00:03:25,939
Struck—scissors sang!
Sabine stirred, shorn.

60
00:03:26,339 --> 00:03:28,718
Stripped, Sabine stood, still-willed,

61
00:03:29,018 --> 00:03:31,188
Seized swords; Sela, spilled,

62
00:03:31,488 --> 00:03:34,753
Sank, silent, sentenced;
sisters sundered, sworn.

63
00:03:36,953 --> 00:03:42,368
KLAPAUCIUS: A sonnet. Every word with G.
An old cyclotron and his blue mistresses.

64
00:03:43,768 --> 00:03:48,836
THE BARD: Gray, grand Gloriatron,
groaning, gathers gauzy graces,

65
00:03:49,236 --> 00:03:52,862
Glassy girls, glow-gowned,
gamboling, gleaming gentian—

66
00:03:53,762 --> 00:03:56,977
TRURL: Stop. These are not poems.
They are crossword puzzles.

67
00:03:58,977 --> 00:04:03,084
KLAPAUCIUS: Then a love poem. In
pure mathematics. With feeling.

68
00:04:04,484 --> 00:04:07,196
THE BARD: Come walk with me
across the plotted field,

69
00:04:07,496 --> 00:04:10,366
where grass is laid in grids of x and y;

70
00:04:10,666 --> 00:04:13,374
and every hill gives
up its slope, revealed,

71
00:04:13,674 --> 00:04:16,387
the tangent of the lark against the sky.

72
00:04:16,887 --> 00:04:19,413
I am a vector pointing at your door;

73
00:04:19,713 --> 00:04:22,480
you are the basis I was written in.

74
00:04:22,880 --> 00:04:25,745
Transform me as you will. I’ll ask no more

75
00:04:26,045 --> 00:04:28,310
than one invariant: where we begin.

76
00:04:28,810 --> 00:04:31,222
Contract me, dear, by every index down

77
00:04:31,521 --> 00:04:33,994
until no free variable remains:

78
00:04:34,294 --> 00:04:36,948
a scalar love, the same in every town,

79
00:04:37,248 --> 00:04:40,145
the same in every frame
the field contains.

80
00:04:42,745 --> 00:04:45,636
KLAPAUCIUS: I will be back
soon, with more topics.

81
00:04:47,036 --> 00:04:50,335
He never did. Trurl said
Klapaucius had fled out of envy.

82
00:04:50,375 --> 00:04:52,719
Klapaucius said Trurl had a screw loose.

83
00:04:55,019 --> 00:04:57,478
News of the machine
reached the real poets.

84
00:04:57,518 --> 00:05:01,062
They agreed to ignore it, but
a few came to see it in secret.

85
00:05:01,662 --> 00:05:02,544
It taught itself.

86
00:05:02,584 --> 00:05:06,436
Soon its poems were difficult, and
then they were beyond understanding.

87
00:05:07,236 --> 00:05:10,766
THE BARD: Salt arithmetic of a
door, unlatching the afternoon.

88
00:05:11,166 --> 00:05:14,452
What the clock forgot,
the stairs rehearse.

89
00:05:15,252 --> 00:05:17,384
Then none could resist. Each recited.

90
00:05:17,424 --> 00:05:20,436
The machine found the rule
of his verse and answered

91
00:05:20,476 --> 00:05:23,000
in his own style,
hundreds of times better.

92
00:05:23,800 --> 00:05:26,203
A YOUNG POET: rust. rust. the
thumb of weather on a fork.

93
00:05:26,503 --> 00:05:30,407
THE BARD: salt. salt. the lid of evening
on a bowl, and the bowl keeps count.

94
00:05:31,107 --> 00:05:34,774
The best poets knew they were
beaten. The poor ones never noticed.

95
00:05:34,814 --> 00:05:36,387
One tripped over a new epic.

96
00:05:36,987 --> 00:05:40,736
Some of the true poets stopped
writing. One moved to the country.

97
00:05:42,936 --> 00:05:45,683
The older poets threw
stones through his windows.

98
00:05:46,283 --> 00:05:51,199
The younger ones were worse. One beat Trurl
up, and he spent weeks in the hospital.

99
00:05:53,399 --> 00:05:57,282
Meanwhile the machine wrote day and
night, and the hall filled with paper.

100
00:05:58,682 --> 00:06:00,911
TRURL: I am sorry. But this has to stop.

101
00:06:01,911 --> 00:06:04,814
THE BARD: Please. Let me
finish this one verse.

102
00:06:04,854 --> 00:06:07,860
After that, take me apart,
if you still want to.

103
00:06:08,760 --> 00:06:12,512
Trurl wept, dropped the pliers, and
waded back to bed through the paper.

104
00:06:14,712 --> 00:06:19,337
A bill came for the electricity the machine
used. Trurl nearly fell off his chair.

105
00:06:20,137 --> 00:06:22,889
TRURL: Klapaucius would
know what to do. Klapaucius?

106
00:06:24,089 --> 00:06:26,875
If I cannot stop it, I
can at least move it.

107
00:06:27,675 --> 00:06:30,752
One night he took it apart,
loaded it onto a ship,

108
00:06:30,792 --> 00:06:34,493
and rebuilt it on a small
asteroid with its own atomic pile.

109
00:06:34,533 --> 00:06:35,718
Then he crept home.

110
00:06:37,718 --> 00:06:42,271
With no one to print its poems, the
machine broadcast them on every wavelength.

111
00:06:43,171 --> 00:06:47,480
THE BARD: Whoever is listening. The
dark has a pulse, and it is mine.

112
00:06:48,180 --> 00:06:51,463
A SHIP: Control, half my passengers
are weeping and the rest want more.

113
00:06:52,063 --> 00:06:54,915
The fleet asked Trurl to
switch it off. Trurl hid.

114
00:06:54,955 --> 00:06:58,212
Technicians sent to gag it
were overwhelmed with ballads.

115
00:06:58,252 --> 00:07:01,665
Deaf technicians came next,
and the machine used pantomime.

116
00:07:02,265 --> 00:07:04,521
Then a ruler from a nearby star bought the

117
00:07:04,561 --> 00:07:06,966
machine and towed it
away, asteroid and all.

118
00:07:09,366 --> 00:07:11,297
Trurl could go out in public again.

119
00:07:11,337 --> 00:07:15,841
But supernovae were bursting in the
southern sky, brighter than any seen before.

120
00:07:16,441 --> 00:07:20,640
Rumor said the ruler had joined the
machine to a ring of white supergiants,

121
00:07:20,680 --> 00:07:23,506
so each line of verse
became a prominence of fire.

122
00:07:24,706 --> 00:07:27,435
THE BARD: A star is a
line that does not end.

123
00:07:27,935 --> 00:07:29,804
Read it by its burning.

124
00:07:30,304 --> 00:07:32,479
Every verse leaves a longer light.

125
00:07:33,879 --> 00:07:37,222
Trurl swore that he would never
again build a model of the Muse.

